9905 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069906 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069906 #edb/ref 1061 655421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f13b1e1c-2034-5d26-b6a0-2b5227d65b96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069907 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069907 #edb/ref 1061 655424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069907 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c22a3f17-9151-565b-8158-62703c1da221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069907 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069908 #edb/ref 1061 655427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069908 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fbb28f81-ea51-5172-9911-15b8c5ea0ef5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069908 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070031 #edb/ref 1064 "We know that we can use the method of integration by parts to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ Now, suppose that we want to calculate $\\displaystyle \\int x^2e^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ Can we use integration by parts in this case?\n\nLet's  try. We define $u$ and $v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the formula for integration by parts leads to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe still don't have an answer, but the problem is reduced to the calculation of a simpler integral  $\\displaystyle \\int xe^{4x} \\textrm{d}x.$ To compute this integral, we just need to apply the integration by parts formula again. For this second integral, we take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{4x} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {e}^{4x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})-\\int (\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,{e}^{4x})+{C}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is a constant of integration.\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{4}\\,x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{16}\\,{e}^{4x}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}-\\frac{1}{8}\\,x{e}^{4x}+\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}-\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\,{e}^{4x}(8{x}^{2}-4x+1)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -\\dfrac{C_1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070031 #edb/ref 1065 901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070031 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "35a2eb7b-569b-5939-bcc0-2261596617e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070031 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186082776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186082777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186082778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186082779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186082780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186082775 #edb/ref 1076 #e